course: dictionary
lesson: 12071
title: Group words and their meaning
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Clergy is a word for a group. It means the priests, ministers, vicars, rabbis, imams and other religious leaders of a religion, all taken together. One vicar on her own is not "the clergy", but she is a member of it.
explain: «The clergy led the prayers at the village church every Sunday.» Look at the clues around the word: prayers, church and Sunday all point to worship. So the clergy are the religious leaders who lead worship.
explain: «In the Middle Ages, the clergy were one of the most powerful groups, along with the nobles and the peasants.» Here the clergy are a group of people in history, standing beside the nobles and the peasants. The sentence shows that the word is about a whole group, not one person.
fact: Clergy means the religious leaders of a religion, as a group.
fact: Members of the clergy are people whose job is to lead worship and look after a religious community.
fact: Priests, ministers, vicars, rabbis and imams are all part of the clergy.
fact: We often write «the clergy», «members of the clergy» or «local clergy».
fact: In medieval times, the clergy were a powerful group, along with the nobles and the peasants.
exercise: «The clergy led the prayers at the village church every Sunday.» Who are the clergy in this sentence? | *Religious leaders who lead worship | Children from the local school | Farmers who grow the food for the village | Shopkeepers who sell bread
exercise: Which of these is a member of the clergy? | A bus driver | *A vicar | A baker | A postman
exercise: Mia says clergy means one single priest. What is the answer? | *No, clergy means a group of religious leaders | No, clergy means a type of church building | Yes, clergy always means one person | Yes, clergy means a holy book
test: «In the Middle Ages, the clergy were one of the most powerful groups, along with the nobles and the peasants.» Which other groups does the sentence name? | Shopkeepers and bakers | Teachers and nurses | Soldiers and sailors | *Nobles and peasants
test: What does clergy mean? | A kind of church building | A song sung at Sunday services | A holy book | *The religious leaders of a religion, as a group
exam: Which sentence uses clergy correctly? | The clergy is a small bird that sings at dawn | *The local clergy visited our school to talk about their work | Clergy means a rainy day in the summer | I went to the clergy to buy a loaf of bread
